\documentclass{article}
\title{Distillation for Protein Contact Prediction}
\author{Anon}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Protein structure prediction has advanced rapidly, but large models are expensive to run at proteome scale. Contact prediction offers a lighter proxy for structure. We study whether a small student model can inherit the accuracy of a large teacher on this task.
\section{Methods}
We distill a 600M-parameter teacher into a 30M-parameter student. The student is trained on sequence pairs with masked language modeling and contact-map regression. We add an attention-transfer loss that aligns student attention maps with the teacher's. Training uses two public corpora and runs for three days on eight GPUs.
\section{Results}
The student retains 97 percent of the teacher's top-L contact precision while running 15 times faster at inference. Attention transfer contributes a 2-point gain over plain distillation. On a held-out set of membrane proteins the student degrades gracefully.
\section{Discussion}
Distillation trades a small accuracy drop for a large speedup.
\end{document}
